      <h2>Building an Honest Profile That Attracts the Right Person</h2>

      <p>
        On any platform where profiles matter, the instinct to present an idealized version of
        yourself is understandable but usually counterproductive. A profile built around
        exaggeration attracts people who are interested in the exaggeration, not in you — which
        means early excitement tends to collapse quickly once real conversation starts.
      </p>

      <h3>Be Specific, Not Generic</h3>

      <p>
        "I like music and travel" describes almost everyone and tells a reader nothing memorable.
        "I've been slowly learning to play badly on a secondhand guitar for two years" gives someone
        an actual detail to respond to, and it filters for people who find that detail appealing
        rather than merely tolerable.
      </p>

      <h3>Say What You're Looking For, Plainly</h3>

      <p>
        Vague profiles ("open to whatever happens") tend to attract mismatched expectations.
        Being clear that you're looking for a relationship — not just casual chat — saves both
        people time and reduces the chance of one person investing emotionally while the other
        isn't on the same page.
      </p>

      <h3>Leave Room for a Real Person to Show Up</h3>

      <p>
        A profile that reads like a highlight reel with no ordinary details can feel intimidating
        or hard to relate to. Small, unremarkable specifics — an opinion, a routine, something
        you're bad at — tend to make people feel like they're talking to an actual person rather
        than a curated pitch.
      </p>

      <h2>Turning a Match Into a Real Connection</h2>

      <p>
        Matching with someone, or having one good conversation, is the easy part. What actually
        determines whether it goes anywhere is a smaller set of conversational habits.
      </p>

      <h3>Ask Questions You're Genuinely Curious About</h3>

      <p>
        Scripted questions ("what do you do for fun?") tend to get scripted answers. Following up
        on something the other person actually said — asking why they picked that hobby, or what
        got them into it — signals real attention and usually leads to a more natural exchange.
      </p>

      <h3>Let Conversations Build Instead of Racing to Depth</h3>

      <p>
        There's a temptation, especially in early online conversations, to move quickly into
        deeply personal territory to "test" a connection. This can actually short-circuit trust
        rather than build it. Comfortable depth tends to come from several lighter conversations
        first, not from one intense one.
      </p>

      <h3>Notice How the Conversation Feels, Not Just What's Said</h3>

      <p>
        Compatibility shows up as much in rhythm — do you both enjoy the pacing, do silences feel
        comfortable or awkward, does the humor land — as it does in shared interests on paper.
        These signals are worth paying attention to, even though they're harder to describe than
        a checklist of compatible traits.
      </p>

      <h2>How an Online Connection Can Develop Over Time</h2>

